8. EMPIRICAL VALIDATION
8.1 Data Source
Phase 10.5 used real recursive trajectories generated from Phase 11.

Parameter	Value
Ontologies	2
State Dimension	16
Horizons	100, 200, 400, 600
Trajectories	2
Seeds	1
State Samples	~200 per horizon
8.2 Trajectory Collection Correction
A major implementation issue was discovered during early validation. Initial snapshots contained only 1 state per trajectory, producing unstable occupancy estimates.

This caused:

Numerical instability

Invalid coverage estimation

Unreliable correlations

Correction: Trajectory windows of ~100 states per horizon were introduced.

This dramatically improved:

Occupancy stability

Coverage estimation

Geometric consistency

9. EMPIRICAL RESULTS
9.1 Coverage Estimates
Ensemble coverage estimates:

Horizon	Coverage
100	0.6189
200	0.6213
400	0.6056
600	0.6123
Observed behavior:

Coverage stabilizes

Occupancy growth compresses

Full occupancy is NOT required

9.2 Coverage–Novelty Correlation
Observed correlation:

r
=
−
0.5128
r=−0.5128

Interpretation: Higher occupancy is associated with lower novelty. This was one of the strongest empirical findings of Phase 10.5.

9.3 Bootstrap Analysis
Bootstrap resampling (n=1000):

Metric	Value
Valid Resamples	982
Mean Correlation	-0.4747
Std Correlation	0.6117
Probability(corr < 0)	85.2%
Interpretation: Directional negative correlation evidence is strong under exploratory conditions. However, sample size remains too small for confirmatory statistical inference.

10. TRAJECTORY DIAGNOSTICS
10.1 Effective Dimensionality
Metric	Value
Effective Dimension (90% variance)	9.0
Participation Ratio	3.91
Interpretation: Recursive exploration occupies significantly lower-dimensional subregions than the nominal 16-dimensional space. This became one of the most important discoveries for Phase 10.6.

10.2 Revisit Dynamics
Metric	Value
Mean Revisit Rate	1.0000
Interpretation: Recursive trajectories repeatedly revisit previously occupied regions. This strongly supports occupancy accumulation dynamics.

10.3 Density Structure
Metric	Value
Dense Region Fraction	0.9958
Sparse Region Fraction	0.0000
Interpretation: Exploration strongly concentrates into dense occupancy regions. This suggests attractor-like representational confinement.

11. PHASE-STRUCTURED EXPLORATION DISCOVERY
The most important discovery of Phase 10.5 was: novelty exhibits phase structure.

Phase	Horizon Range	Novelty Range	Behavior
Expansion	0 → 400	0.085 → 0.151	Novelty rises rapidly
Peak	~400	0.151	Exploration diversity maximizes
Compression	400 → 600+	0.151 → 0.134	Occupancy buildup constrains novelty
This replaced the earlier monotonic saturation interpretation.

